course: dictionary
lesson: 3802
title: Forecast: a good guess about the future
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A forecast is a statement of what will probably happen in the future, especially about the weather. «The forecast says showers later.» The word probably is the clue: a forecast is a good guess based on information, not a certainty.
explain: Forecast can also be a verb, meaning to say what you think will happen using information. Forecasters use measurements, maps and computers to forecast rain, wind and temperature. Their «weather forecasts» are carefully reasoned guesses.
explain: «Weather forecasts use computer models and AI to make good guesses, but weather is hard to predict, so forecasts are not promises.» So a forecast can sometimes be wrong. In a sentence, ask yourself: is this a promise, or a best guess?
fact: A forecast is a statement of what will probably happen in the future.
fact: «Forecast» can be a noun (a forecast) or a verb (to forecast): to say what will happen, using information.
fact: Forecasters use measurements, maps and computers to predict rain, wind and temperature.
fact: «Satellites» help us forecast the weather, and the International Space Station is a large satellite about 400 kilometres up.
fact: A forecast is a good guess, not a promise, because weather is hard to predict.
exercise: «The forecast says showers later, so pack a raincoat.» What does forecast mean here? | A map of the country | A holiday by the sea | A sudden storm that has already arrived | *What will probably happen in the future, such as the weather
exercise: Ella wants to forecast tomorrow's rain. What does she want to do? | To stop the rain from falling | *To say what she thinks will happen, using information | To hide inside the house | To buy a new umbrella
exercise: Why are forecasts not promises? | Because forecasts are written in a book | *Because weather is hard to predict, so they can be wrong | Because the weather always does exactly what is predicted | Because forecasters never use computers
test: «Satellites are used for weather forecasts, phone calls, television, maps and finding the way.» Which use is NOT listed? | Television | Weather forecasts | *Cooking dinner | Finding the way
test: A boy in Dublin packs a raincoat and a jumper. Why? | Because he is going swimming in the sea | *Because the forecast says showers later | Because his jumper is already wet | Because the sun is hot all day
exam: Which best explains why a forecast is not a promise? | A forecast is written down in a book | *Weather is hard to predict, so a forecast is a good guess | The forecast is always right | Forecasters never use computers
